WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
Shared mutable state is data that more than one operation can read or write.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:16.200
It may be an in-memory field, a static cache, a collection, a database row, a file, a queue message, a counter, a session record, or a business workflow state.

3
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:20.620
The state does not have to live in memory to create a concurrency problem.

4
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:26.900
A race condition occurs when correctness depends on timing or interleaving between operations.

5
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:35.960
If one operation reads a value, another changes it, and the first continues as if the old value is still current, the application may violate its own rules.

6
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.940
These defects are hard to reproduce because a small timing change can hide or reveal the issue.

7
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.580
Read-modify-write sequences are especially sensitive.

8
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:52.280
Checking a value and then changing it is often intended to be one logical operation, but the machine may perform it as multiple steps.

9
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:59.900
Without a synchronization rule, two operations can both observe the same starting state and both make decisions that were only valid for one operation.

10
00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.820
Invariants are the statements that must remain true even under concurrent access.

11
00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.080
An account balance should not be derived from missing transactions.

12
00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.500
A job should not be completed twice.

13
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.280
A quota should not become negative.

14
00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:17.460
A cache should not expose partially initialized objects.

15
00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:22.120
Naming the invariant helps the team decide which operations must be protected together.

16
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.200
The best synchronization is often less sharing.

17
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:36.420
Immutability, ownership, message passing, local variables, snapshots, narrow scope, and clear state transitions all reduce the number of places where timing matters.

18
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:45.080
If only one component owns the state, other components can interact through a controlled interface instead of reaching into the data directly.

19
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.600
Document which code owns a piece of state and which rule protects it.

20
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:57.860
That rule might be a lock, a database transaction, a queue ordering guarantee, an atomic operation, a channel, or an immutable snapshot.

21
00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.120
Reviewers should not have to infer the ownership model from scattered reads and writes.
